<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  List of files to be loaded into which enum type
      This is used both for xml reading at startup and by autogenerate to set headers
      Be aware that this file has no schema and as such tags can appear in any order. Try to keep the file clean anyway.
  
  Files:
    the file paths (max one file per enum at the time of writing)
  Type:
    error messages will report the text in the child tag of this name. Defaults to Type
  Types:
    list of vanilla types. Fallback in case autogenerate.exe can't locate the vanilla files
  InfoSource:
    Enum to set where the info array is stored. Valid values GC, INFO and ENUMMAP. Auto configured values should work in most cases
  GetInfo:
    Name of the function to get info from GC (mostly self configuring)
  InfoClass:
    Name of the class storing the info (mostly self configuring)
  HardcodingType:
    Enum to set how to generate the header files. Valid options:
     - TYPE_STANDARD:  uses hardcoded/dynamic set at compile time (default)
     - TYPE_DYNAMIC:   enums will never be hardcoded, not even in hardcoded dll files
     - TYPE_HARDCODED: only uses hardcoded enums even in dynamic dlls (switch case can only be used on hardcoded enums)
     - TYPE_NO_INFO:   hardcoded and Type class will not be able to provide xml info
     - TYPE_NO_FILE:   hardcoded and DLL will not read the xml at runtime. (default if the Files tag is obmitted)
     - TYPE_PLAYER:    used for player and team. Sets length to MAX_PLAYERS. No xml file linking

         Main difference between TYPE_NO_INFO and TYPE_NO_FILE is that only TYPE_NO_INFO will test hardcoded length against xml length at startup.
         Both are useful for setting enums in xml, but nothing is needed other than the Type for the enums

         NOTE: all types can be used in tags in xml files regardless of HardcodingType setting
-->
<Files>
	<AreaAITypes>
